snip">Ammianus Marcellinus was a Greek-born Roman soldier and historian.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammishtamru-i"><span class="enc-name">Ammishtamru I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammishtamru I was king of Ugarit in the fourteenth century BCE, during the Amarna period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammishtamru-ii"><span class="enc-name">Ammishtamru II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammishtamru II was king of Ugarit in the thirteenth century BCE, ruling as a vassal of the Hittite Empire at the height of the city's prosperity.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammonius-saccas"><span class="enc-name">Ammonius Saccas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammonius Saccas was a Greek philosopher who taught in Alexandria in the first half of the third century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammunira"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ammunira.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ammunira</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ammunira was the ruler of Beirut in the mid-14th century BCE, a vassal of the Egyptian pharaoh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amnisos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amnisos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amnisos</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amnisos is an ancient harbour site on the north coast of Crete, east of Heraklion, that served as a port of Knossos.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amnon-ben-tor"><span class="enc-name">Amnon Ben-Tor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amnon Ben-Tor is an Israeli archaeologist and professor at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem who has directed renewed excavations at Tel Hazor since 1990.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amoghabhuti"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amoghabhuti.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amoghabhuti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amoghabhuti was a king of the Kunindas, a people of the Himalayan foothills, who reigned around the 1st century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amoghapasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amoghapasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amoghapasa</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Amoghapasa is a form of the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara in Mahayana and Vajrayana Buddhism, whose name refers to his unfailing noose for saving beings.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amoghavarsha-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amoghavarsha I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amoghavarsha I was the longest-reigning Rashtrakuta emperor, ruling the Deccan from 814 to 878.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-iii"><span class="enc-name">Amoghavarsha III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amoghavarsha III, also called Baddega, was Rashtrakuta emperor of the Deccan from about 936 to 939.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amol"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amol.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amol</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amol is a city in Mazandaran province, northern Iran, on the Haraz river near the Caspian coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amon-of-judah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amon-of-judah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amon of Judah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amon was king of Judah for about two years in the mid-7th century BCE, the son and successor of Manasseh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amor-de-cosmos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amor-de-cosmos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amor De Cosmos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amor De Cosmos, born William Alexander Smith in Nova Scotia, was a journalist and politician in British Columbia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amorbach"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amorbach.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amorbach</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amorbach is a small town in the Odenwald in Lower Franconia, Bavaria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amorgos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amorgos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amorgos</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amorgos is a Greek island in the south-eastern Cyclades.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amorite-wall"><span class="enc-name">Amorite Wall</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Amorite Wall was a defensive wall built by kings of the Third Dynasty of Ur in Mesopotamia to keep out Amorite groups migrating from the west.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amorites"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amorites.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amorites</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Amorites were a Semitic-speaking people of ancient Syria and Mesopotamia, originally seen by Mesopotamian sources as nomadic outsiders.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amos-prophet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amos-prophet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amos (prophet)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amos was a Hebrew prophet of the 8th century BCE, a herdsman from Tekoa in Judah who preached in the northern Kingdom of Israel under Jeroboam II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amphilochian-argos"><span class="enc-name">Amphilochian Argos</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amphilochian Argos was an ancient Greek city at the south-eastern corner of the Ambracian Gulf, in western Greece, the chief town of the Amphilochians.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amphora"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amphora.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amphora</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">An amphora is a two-handled ceramic jar used in the ancient Mediterranean world to transport and store goods such as wine and olive oil.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amr-ibn-al-as.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amr ibn al-As</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amr ibn al-As was an Arab commander and companion of the Prophet Muhammad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amritsar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amritsar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amritsar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amritsar is a city in the Punjab region of north-western India and the spiritual centre of Sikhism, home to the Harmandir Sahib, or Golden Temple.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amshuvarman"><span class="enc-name">Amshuvarman</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Amshuvarman was a ruler of Nepal in the Licchavi period, first a powerful minister under King Shivadeva I and then holder of royal titles.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/amsivarii"><span class="enc-name">Amsivarii</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Amsivarii were a Germanic people living near the river Ems in north-western Germany in the 1st century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amsterdam.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amsterdam</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Amsterdam is the capital of the Netherlands and was the commercial and financial heart of the Dutch Republic in the seventeenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amuktamalyada"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amuktamalyada.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amuktamalyada</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Amuktamalyada is a Telugu epic poem composed by the Vijayanagara emperor Krishnadevaraya in the early sixteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/amulius"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/amulius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Amul